  "activity": {
    "id": "act_dna_structure_replication_linear",
    "name": "DNA Double Helix and Semiconservative Replication",
    "description": "A simplified, linear AR lab for 200-level biology and biochemistry majors. Students progress from a flat DNA ladder schematic to a three-dimensional double helix, identify base-pairing and antiparallel directionality, then trace the enzyme-driven replication fork and explain leading, lagging, Okazaki fragment, ligase, and semiconservative outcomes.",
    "workplace": "wp_dna_replication_intro",
    "start": "step_01_ladder_structure",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_01_ladder_structure",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "1. Identify DNA\u2019s basic parts",
          "description": "Examine the flat DNA ladder. The vertical rails represent sugar-phosphate backbones, and the rungs represent paired nitrogenous bases projecting inward. Learning objective: identify backbone, bases, and hydrogen bonds as structural components of DNA."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "continue_to_helix"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_02_double_helix",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "2. Map the ladder onto the double helix",
          "description": "Watch the ladder twist into a double helix. The sugar-phosphate backbones spiral around a common axis while base pairs stack inside the helix. Learning objective: describe how DNA components assemble into the double helix."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "detect_helix_marker"
          },
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "continue_to_antiparallel"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_03_antiparallel_direction",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "3. Explain antiparallel directionality",
          "description": "Use the arrows and 5\u2032/3\u2032 labels to compare the two backbones. One strand runs 5\u2032\u21923\u2032 while the complementary strand runs 3\u2032\u21925\u2032. Learning objective: explain how sugar-phosphate directionality creates antiparallel DNA."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "continue_to_pairing"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_04_complementary_pairing",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "4. Apply complementary base-pairing rules",
          "description": "Complete the complementary strand for the template 5\u2032-ATGCCAGT-3\u2032. A pairs with T through two hydrogen bonds; G pairs with C through three. Learning objective: determine a complementary sequence and connect pairing specificity to hydrogen bonds."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "detect_base_palette"
          },
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "continue_to_helicase"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_05_helicase_and_ssb",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "5. Open and stabilize the replication fork",
          "description": "Helicase unwinds DNA by disrupting hydrogen bonds. Single-strand binding proteins then hold the separated templates open. Learning objective: identify helicase and SSB functions at the replication fork."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "detect_replication_fork"
          },
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "continue_to_polymerase"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_06_leading_strand",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "6. Follow continuous leading-strand synthesis",
          "description": "DNA polymerase extends only from a 3\u2032 end, so the new strand is synthesized 5\u2032\u21923\u2032. On the leading-strand template, polymerase can move continuously with the fork. Learning objective: relate polymerase directionality to continuous leading-strand synthesis."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "continue_to_lagging"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_07_lagging_ligase",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "7. Build lagging strand fragments and seal them",
          "description": "Because the opposite template is antiparallel, polymerase synthesizes short Okazaki fragments away from the fork. DNA ligase joins fragment junctions into a continuous strand. Learning objective: explain why lagging-strand synthesis is discontinuous and identify ligase function."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "continue_to_semiconservative"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_08_semiconservative",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "8. Predict semiconservative products",
          "description": "Observe two replication rounds. After one round, each daughter has one parental strand and one new strand. After two rounds, two molecules remain hybrid and two are fully new. Learning objective: explain semiconservative replication and predict daughter-molecule composition."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "detect_semiconservative_model"
          },
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "continue_to_recap"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_09_recap",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "9. Recap the conceptual chain",
          "description": "Review the structural and mechanistic logic: DNA\u2019s antiparallel double helix determines polymerase directionality, which causes leading and lagging synthesis, producing semiconservative daughter molecules. Learning objective: synthesize the full explanation from structure to replication outcome."
        },
        "enter": {
